  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "past simple: negative didn't",
      "title_form": "Pasado simple: negativo con didn't",
      "title_target": "Past simple: negative with didn't",
      "description_md_form": "Para decir que **no** hiciste algo en el pasado, usamos **didn't** (did not) + el **verbo en forma base**. El verbo NO cambia: no se agrega -ed ni se usa la forma irregular.\n\n**Estructura:** `Subject + didn't + verb (base form)`\n\n**Ejemplos:**\n- `I didn't accept the job.` = No acepté el trabajo.\n- `She didn't mention the meeting.` = Ella no mencionó la reunión.\n\n**Puntos clave:**\n- **didn't** ya indica pasado, así que el verbo principal queda en infinitivo.\n- Con **nothing** y **none**, la oración es afirmativa pero el significado es negativo: `He ate nothing.` = Él no comió nada.\n- Con **anything**, usamos **didn't**: `I didn't buy anything.` = No compré nada.",
      "description_md_target": "To say that you **did not** do something in the past, use **didn't** (did not) + the **base form of the verb**. The verb does NOT change: no -ed and no irregular form.\n\n**Structure:** `Subject + didn't + verb (base form)`\n\n**Examples:**\n- `I didn't accept the job.`\n- `She didn't mention the meeting.`\n\n**Key points:**\n- **didn't** already shows past, so the main verb stays in the base form.\n- With **nothing** and **none**, the sentence is affirmative but the meaning is negative: `He ate nothing.`\n- With **anything**, use **didn't**: `I didn't buy anything.`",
      "grammar_structure": "Subject + didn't + verb (base form)",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "past_simple_negative",
        "headers": [
          "Pronombre",
